<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Illyrian Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">400 BCE – 168 BCE (approx.) · capital Scodra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of the Illyrians on the eastern Adriatic, ruled at its height by Agron and Queen Teuta, whose piracy provoked Rome's first wars across the Adriatic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Illyrian kingdoms appear from about 400 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardylis">Bardylis</a> built a power that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in 393 and 359 BCE. In the 3rd century BCE the Ardiaean dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a> united the coast from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scodra</a> (Shkodër) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/risan">Rhizon</a> (Risan) as royal seats.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled through tribal chiefs and depended on a strong fleet of light lembi, used for war and for raiding Adriatic and Ionian shipping. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> in 231 BCE. Royal coins were struck at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scodra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lissus</a>. Greek cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stari-grad">Pharos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-dalmatia">Issa</a> lay on its edges.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta's</a> attacks on Italian traders led to the First Illyrian War in 229 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-pharos">Demetrius of Pharos</a> provoked a second in 219 BCE. In the Third Illyrian War of 168 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> captured King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentius">Gentius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scodra</a> and split the kingdom, which later became the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyricum-roman-province">Illyricum</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/illyrian-kingdom.jpg" alt="Illyrian Kingdom" width="320" height="309"><figcaption>Image: Nishjan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_the_Ardiaei,_late_3rd_century_B.C.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 BCE – 168 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Scodra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Agron, Teuta, Gentius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Illyrian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Illyrian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Illyria, Ardiaean Kingdom, Labeatan Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-284&amp;focus=illyrian-kingdom" aria-label="Illyrian Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5154 680 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Illyrian Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5474 936L5304 942M5474 936L5475 960M5474 936L5416 887"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5475 960h0M5416 887h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5474 936h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5474 936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Scodra. <a href="/atlas?year=-284&amp;focus=illyrian-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
